---
# yamllint disable rule:line-length
# The primitive twin of `216-recognize-distinct` (#996). `216` pins
# `java/util/stream/Stream` on both the call and the pragma, so an
# `IntStream.distinct()` never became a pragma at all: it stayed a native call
# and split the pipeline into two independently fused runs around it.
#
# Every other fusible operation already had its primitive twin — `203`/`232`
# for filter, `204`/`231` for map, `208`/`233` for dropWhile since #982 — and
# each of them goes through the same boxing sandwich `211` builds: a
# `Φ.hone.box` in front, the operation in the middle, a `Φ.hone.unbox` behind.
# This rule mints the primitive pragma; `214-box-unbox-primitive-stateful`
# builds the sandwich around it and puts `216`'s own pragma in the middle, so
# `307` folds it unchanged. The two steps are separate for the reason `210`
# keeps them separate for dropWhile: `213` reverts a pragma on a parallel
# pipeline BEFORE anything is boxed, so such a pipeline never enters the
# sandwich and ends up exactly the native call it started as.
#
# `211` cannot do the boxing itself, which is why `214` exists: it derives the
# unbox from the lifted lambda's `bridge-signature`, and distinct carries no
# lambda — there is no signature to read, and the element type is already fixed
# by the stream interface.
#
# `IntStream` ONLY, still, and the reason is no longer the slot layout (#1012
# widened that) but #1016: `214`'s sandwich ends in an unbox, and when the
# user's own `boxed()` follows the operation immediately, `221` cancels that
# unbox against it and keeps only the unbox — the re-boxing the user asked for
# disappears and the run hands a `mapMultiToInt`'s IntStream to code holding
# the `Stream` `boxed()` promised. That is live on `IntStream` today, so lifting
# `LongStream` and `DoubleStream` here would not introduce the defect, only
# widen its reach, and `RandomPipeline` seed 18 walks straight into it. The two
# TYPE-TRANSPARENT operations are the only ones exposed: `231`/`232`/`233` give
# their pragmas a boxed bridge with only `accepted` and `returned` primitive, so
# `411`/`422` put the re-boxing back, which is why `dropWhile` on a wide element
# fuses (#1012) and these two wait for #1016.
#
# NUMBERED 212, not beside `216`, because `214`'s sandwich must land after
# `209-unbox-box-to-distill`, which folds a user `mapToX(...).boxed()`
# round-trip and whose header spells out why a synthetic box must never be in
# the tree when it runs, and after `211`, so the sandwich is one `221` can
# cancel against its neighbours like any other.
#
# The pattern matches at class scope to capture the enclosing class name, which
# `307` needs on the resulting distill so `401-fuse` can compose it with the
# neighbouring distills (they share one `class`), and which the unbox needs as
# the class that will own its synthesised lambda.
name: recognize-primitive-distinct
pattern: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-invokeinterface ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.jeo.opcode.invokeinterface,
          𝜏1 ↦ 𝑒-stream-class,
          𝜏2 ↦ "distinct",
          𝜏3 ↦ 𝑒-signature,
          𝜏4 ↦ Φ.true,
          ρ ↦ ∅
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail,
    ρ ↦ ∅
  ⟧
result: |
  ⟦
    φ ↦ Φ.jeo.class,
    𝐵-class-head-before,
    name ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
    𝐵-class-head-after,
    𝜏-caller ↦ ⟦
      φ ↦ Φ.jeo.method,
      𝐵-caller-head,
      body ↦ ⟦
        𝐵-body-head,
        𝜏-distinct ↦ ⟦
          φ ↦ Φ.hone.primitive-distinct,
          class ↦ 𝑒-class-name,
          stream-class ↦ 𝑒-stream-class
        ⟧,
        𝐵-body-tail
      ⟧,
      𝐵-caller-tail
    ⟧,
    𝐵-class-tail,
    ρ ↦ ∅
  ⟧
where:
  - meta: 𝜏-distinct
    function: random-tau
  - meta: 𝑒-signature
    function: concat
    args: ['"()L"', 𝑒-stream-class, '";"']
when:
  and:
    - eq: [𝑒-stream-class, '"java/util/stream/IntStream"']
